  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 Days...\n\nA weak and inconsequential cold front will push through the CWA \nbefore sunrise today which will do nothing more than shift winds \nout of the NW. While 700mb winds will be in the 30kt range over \nthe plains, the nocturnal inversion will keep gusts of this\nmagnitude off of the ground. \n\nThe ridge centered over the Great Plains will remain the dominant\nplayer in our weather through much of the week. This feature will\never so slightly build westwards over the next day or two which \nwill put all of our CWA under the broad-scale subsidence. This \nwill also mean that the mid and upper level jet will be oriented \nSW to NE over western MT keeping any threat for a major wind \nevent minimal as well. These winds aloft do not even appear to be\nvery strong on any model. Even though another week cold front \ncould pass through the area Tuesday into Wednesday, the main story\nthis week, if you can even call it that, will be the slightly \nabove normal temperatures. The NBM keeps highs across our \npopulated areas in the high 60s and low 70s through the entirety \nof the work week. \n\nThe next chance for an impactful weather system comes Friday into\nnext weekend. A longwave trough could move ashore over Southern \nCA and push into the Great Basin. There is still a lot of model \ndiscrepancy regarding what this will do for our area. The only \nagreement is that there will be some sort of weather maker. How \nmuch moisture, temperatures, timing, and winds all remain big \nquestions. If you have plans next weekend be sure to check future\nforecasts as the impacts for this system come into better focus.\n \nWMR",
